{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-163556",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14164351/assets/f368d2de12462a1790322edca188232bf8fafe3951facdb29b334e285807b9fe.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(3+kx)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=3+kx.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=3+kx.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (3+kx)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=3x+\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=8\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =3x+\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8)^{2} & =3(0)+\\frac{k(0)^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ C & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=3x+\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{2}+32.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=8\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=8\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =3x+\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{2}+32 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8\\sqrt{2})^{2} & =3(8)+\\frac{k(8)^{2}}{2}+32 \\\\ 64 & =24+32k+32 \\\\ 32k & =8 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
